ngth of time recommended  for unvaccinated asymptomatic household  contacts of confirmed cases  to 10 days from 14 days  aligns with data showing the incubation  period for omicron is shorter than  previous variants  as new evidence emerges we will continue  to review the appropriateness of the  duration of the recommended quarantine  period  our data is indicating that we are  likely beginning to turn a corner with  this fifth wave  while this is encouraging news there are  steps we need to continue to take to  protect ourselves and each other  in particular vaccines remain critically  important  the booster dose has been shown to  strengthen protection against severe  outcomes from omicron including  hospitalization  getting fully immunized against covid19  doesn't just protect an individual it  also helps protect those around them  it helps us all by easing the strain on  hospitals and our frontline healthcare  workers  i also know that due to widespread  transmission there are questions from  albertans about whether they need to be  vaccinated against covid19 or get a  third dose if they have previously been  infected with the virus  i believe the best way to combat  hesitancy is with accurate information  and i would like to answer these  questions  there is evidence that recovery from  covid19 with earlier strains provides  some protection against future  reinfection  but we don't know how strong that  protection is or how long it lasts  the level of protection from  infection-acquired immune responses  will vary in each individual  this level depends on how mild or severe  their prior infection was the time  elapsed since they have recovered from  infection  their age and whether they have a  compromised immune system or other  medical conditions  the evidence is also clear that those  who have previously been infected  and who also have received vaccine have  a significantly stronger level of  protection than recovery from infection  alone  regardless of whether or not an albertan  has antibodies from a previous covid19  infection  getting fully vaccinated with all the  doses we are eligible for is still the  best way to make sure that we and those  around us are protected against severe  illness and outcomes such as  hospitalization due to covid19  there are also questions about how long  people should wait to get their vaccine  after recovering from covid19  there is no set waiting period in  alberta  people who have recovered from covet and  have no contraindications to the vaccine  can choose to receive it as soon as  their isolation period is over  this applies to first second and third  doses  there are advantages and disadvantages  of different timing options  receiving vaccine immediately  or waiting a few weeks or months after  recovery  if you have time if you have questions  about what time frame might be best for  you  i encourage you to speak to a health  care provider and you can also read the  available information on our website  in closing today i want to speak briefly  about the concept of moving to an  endemic approach to covet my colleagues  in other provinces which are farther  ahead in the omicron wave and where  hospitalizations are declining  have been speaking publicly about the  fact that we need to plan and move  towards a different way of managing  covet  i agree with my colleagues that this is  the direction we need to move when we  see similar trends in our acute care  system in alberta  this is a shift in thinking that can be  challenging  given that we have needed to use  extraordinary tools at our disposal over  the past several years to protect our  health system and our communities  what i want to be clear about is what  moving to endemic means  kovid will not go away  there will continue to be impacts on our  acute care systems that will rise and  fall with seasonality and new variants  that may emerge  we cannot prevent all negative outcomes  from covid and we must be ready to  respond to new information  what has been important throughout the  covid response is seeking balance  between the harms of covet and the harms  of the measures needed to prevent an  overwhelming surge of severe outcomes  i believe that after the omicron wave  has subsided the risk of our system  becoming overwhelmed will be  substantially reduced  and this will enable to shift our  response  this change will take time and we need  to continue monitoring severe impacts  and any changes to the virus  but we cannot continue to use  restrictions in the long term once the  risk of system overwhelm has passed  our acute care system is still under  strain today  we need to continue to protect it and by  extension all of us  however it is also true that in the  future we will be able to remove  restrictions and indeed we must continue  to balance our response to minimize all  harms across all areas  the pandemic has never had one single  right way to respond but what is always  true is that the actions that each of us  take every day matter to our communities  thank you and i'm happy to take  questions  due to scheduling today we will need to  wrap up at about 4 p.m so we'll try to  get through as many  questions as we can  please limit yourself to one question  and one follow-up  we have one reporter here in the room so  we'll get started and then move to the  phone  hi dr hinshaw um janet french from the  cbc has the restrictions exemption  program  been achieving its objectives and if so  what objectives do you think it's  achieved  the restrictions exemption program was  put in place in september  in the midst of our fourth wave with a  dramatic surge of delta which was  causing very significant strain on our  acute care system in particular in icus  we saw that  with the implementation of the  restriction exemption program our  vaccine coverage rates went up very  significantly as there was added  incentive for people to get the  protection that vaccine offers to those  who receive it and to those around them  we also saw that in those locations that  had the rep implemented  there were reductions in  the risks of transmission because of the  fact that those who were in those  locations  were fully vaccinated or had that recent  negative test  so the restriction exemption program  has certainly had a significant  beneficial impact we know from our  current data that omicron is escaping  both previous infection protection and  vaccine protection at greater rates than  the delta  variant did but we also have seen  evidence that having two or three doses  does minimize the chance of onward  transmission in someone who has a  breakthrough infection compared to  someone who is unvaccinated so there is  still benefit in  ensuring that we are  protecting our communities through  vaccination  janet do you have a follow-up yeah just  to follow up on that i mean in your mind  would alberta continue to benefit from  having a restriction exemption program  and why or why not  the  decisions about policy including the  length of time the restriction exemption  program is in place  are decisions that are  the purview of elected officials  with respect to the  impact of vaccines again vaccines  continue to be very important they are  important at protecting the individual  who receives that vaccine as well as  those around them  and so i would continue to encourage  albertans to receive all vaccines  they're eligible for as  it's clear that with the omicron wave  the third dose is particularly important  at minimizing the risk of infection and  also the risk of severe outcomes  thank you operator could you please put  through today's first caller  thank you sarah chu city news  hello dr dina my first question is it  connects to the ones that you were just  answered but with hospitalization for  clovis doe hi burnout in the medical  system still present are you on board  with premier kenny saying that the  restrictions exemption program could be  lifted soon  i am not aware of any final decisions  around timing of  lifting of restrictions  i  want to emphasize that as you said the  acute care system is under strain and  it's critical that all of us  play a part in minimizing the spread of  kovidge to protect the system  and that includes all of the actions we  take every day and  choices around vaccine  sarah did you have a follow-up  yes i do so concerning long covet and  how many people can't work full time if  at all because of their debilitating  symptoms would you consider long covert  a disability and would the government  consider creating financial aid to help  sufferers  there is still as you know a lot of  research that we need around the effects  of what's variably called long covid  postcovid syndrome there's a few  different names we're working with  alberta health services on some  different projects to understand the  impacts  in how that plays out in the lives of  individuals who are impacted by it  those people who are suffering from  longer term impacts of kovidge can  access services through alberta health  services in terms of the health care  needs that they have  with respect to financial compensation  for those who are experiencing a medical  condition that renders them unable to  work or to participate in activities the  way they used to  that would be a question that i would  have to  take back with comms and determine  which of the  portfolios might be able to answer that  but  ultimately anyone who's experiencing a  medical condition that  means that they are not able to work  it's my understanding that there are  programs that exist to support people  who are experiencing those kinds of  conditions  thank you operator could you please put  through the next caller  thank you helen pike cbc  hi there thank you so much for speaking  my question i'm just wondering uh i know  that you spoke yesterday i believe about  rapid tests coming into the province um  do you know  uh is that the first kind of delivery  we've had in a while can you kind of  detail  you know how long we've been waiting on  these and how the flow is into the  province now  we can provide more specific details on  volumes and dates uh offline what i can  say is that because of some of the  supply chain challenges that we've  experienced with the increase in demand  globally  that the supplies that we had  anticipated that we would receive in  late december and early january many of  those shipments were delayed to late  january early february so as  you likely know in  the early to mid part of january  what we needed to do was prioritize the  available supplies to health care  settings and schools and this week we  have enough that we're able to begin  restocking pharmacies at the alberta  health services locations that are sites  for distribution of rapid tests as well  as sending additional rapid tests to  first nation communities  we will continue to make rapid tests  available  as long as that supply is  is available to us  uh helen did you have a follow-up  yeah i'm wondering if we are expecting  regular deliveries or if it's still if  we're still in this situation of um you  know kind of waiting around for sporadic  um  supply chain related delays  i think it would be best to follow up  offline just in terms of being able to  give you the exact numbers of what we  have on order the dates that we're  expecting those to arrive  we have different programs there is the  workplace regular asymptomatic screening  program and then we also have the  uh  distribution of kits to those who may  have symptoms and need it to  determine if they have covet or not and  so  there are different volumes in each of  those different programs different  timelines so we'll follow up offline and  make sure you get that information  thank you operator could you put through  the next question please  kevin nimick ctv  hi dr henshaw you said you aren't aware  of a set time to ease restrictions but  